---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "Non-Renewal Lease Letter Template (Free, Attorney-Reviewed, All 50 States)";
const description = "Get a free non-renewal lease letter template. Attorney-reviewed, state-specific, and ready to print or download as a PDF for $14.99. Works for all US states.";
---
<Base {title} {description}>
  <main class="bg-brand-bg py-section-y">
    <article class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6">
      <h1 class="font-heading text-display-xl font-bold text-brand-heading mb-6">Non-Renewal Lease Letter Template: Free, State-Compliant, Ready to Use</h1>

      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]">Most non-renewal lease letter templates floating around online have a problem: they're written for no state in particular, which means they may not meet your state's requirements. A notice that's missing a required element, or sent too late, isn't just ineffective—it can be legally void, forcing you to restart the process. For background on the legal requirements before you fill in this template, read the <a href="/non-renewal-lease-letter/" class="text-brand-cta hover:underline font-semibold">complete non-renewal lease letter guide</a>.</p>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]">This page gives you a working template, explains what each section does, and shows you where a generic template breaks down so you know exactly what to watch for.</p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">What Makes a Non-Renewal Lease Letter Template Valid</h2>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]">A template is only as good as what's in it and how it's completed. The majority of notices that get thrown out in housing court aren't thrown out because the landlord used the wrong form—they're thrown out because the landlord filled in the form incorrectly. Wrong date. Missing unit number. Misspelled tenant name. Too-short notice period.</p>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]"><strong>Six required elements in virtually every state:</strong></p>
      <ol class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-decimal">
        <li><strong>Date of the notice</strong> — Not the date you print it. The date it's being served or sent. This matters for calculating whether you've met the notice deadline.</li>
        <li><strong>Full names of all tenants</strong> — Everyone listed on the lease must be named. Omitting a co-tenant can leave that person without legal notice.</li>
        <li><strong>Full property address</strong> — Include unit number, city, state, and ZIP. This identifies the specific tenancy being ended.</li>
        <li><strong>Unambiguous statement of non-renewal</strong> — The language must clearly state the lease will not be renewed. Vague language doesn't qualify.</li>
        <li><strong>Lease expiration date</strong> — The specific date the current lease ends. This is the date the tenant is expected to vacate.</li>
        <li><strong>Landlord signature and contact information</strong> — Unsigned notices can be challenged.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">Free Non-Renewal Lease Letter Template (Sample)</h2>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]">The following template meets the minimum legal requirements in most US states. <strong>State-specific notice periods and delivery requirements vary—see the state requirements section below before using this letter.</strong></p>

      <div class="my-6 p-6 border border-brand-border rounded-md bg-white font-body text-body-base text-brand-body leading-[1.7]">
        <p><strong>[DATE]</strong></p>
        <br />
        <p><strong>[TENANT FULL NAME(S)]</strong><br />
        <strong>[PROPERTY ADDRESS]</strong><br />
        <strong>[CITY, STATE, ZIP]</strong></p>
        <br />
        <p><strong>Re: Notice of Non-Renewal of Lease Agreement</strong></p>
        <br />
        <p>Dear [Tenant Name(s)],</p>
        <br />
        <p>This letter serves as formal written notice that your lease agreement for the property located at <strong>[FULL PROPERTY ADDRESS INCLUDING UNIT NUMBER]</strong>, originally entered into on <strong>[LEASE START DATE]</strong> and currently expiring on <strong>[LEASE EXPIRATION DATE]</strong>, will <strong>not be renewed</strong>.</p>
        <br />
        <p>You are required to vacate the premises and return possession of the property to the landlord no later than <strong>[MOVE-OUT DATE]</strong>, which is the date your lease expires.</p>
        <br />
        <p>Please ensure that:</p>
        <ul class="pl-6 space-y-1 list-disc my-2">
          <li>All personal belongings are removed from the property by the move-out date</li>
          <li>The property is left in the same condition as when you moved in, less normal wear and tear</li>
          <li>All keys, garage door openers, and access devices are returned to [LANDLORD NAME / PROPERTY MANAGER NAME] at [RETURN ADDRESS / OFFICE ADDRESS]</li>
        </ul>
        <br />
        <p>Your security deposit in the amount of <strong>[$SECURITY DEPOSIT AMOUNT]</strong> is held in accordance with [STATE] law. Following your move-out and a final inspection, any deductions will be itemized and the remaining deposit balance will be returned to you within the timeframe required by [STATE] law.</p>
        <br />
        <p>Please provide your forwarding address at your earliest convenience so we can process your deposit return promptly.</p>
        <br />
        <p>If you have any questions about this notice or the move-out process, please contact me at:</p>
        <br />
        <p><strong>[LANDLORD NAME]</strong><br />
        <strong>[LANDLORD ADDRESS]</strong><br />
        <strong>[PHONE NUMBER]</strong><br />
        <strong>[EMAIL ADDRESS]</strong></p>
        <br />
        <p>Sincerely,</p>
        <br />
        <p>____________________________<br />
        <strong>[LANDLORD / AUTHORIZED AGENT SIGNATURE]</strong></p>
        <br />
        <p><strong>[PRINTED NAME]</strong><br />
        <strong>[DATE SIGNED]</strong></p>
        <br />
        <hr class="border-brand-border my-4" />
        <p><strong>Proof of Service (attach documentation of delivery method used):</strong></p>
        <p>&#9744; Sent via USPS Certified Mail, Tracking #: _______________<br />
        &#9744; Delivered in person on [DATE] at [TIME], witnessed by: _______________<br />
        &#9744; Posted at the property address on [DATE] at [TIME]</p>
        <br />
        <p><em>(This is a general template. Confirm your state's notice requirements before sending.)</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">How to Fill In the Template: Field-by-Field Guide</h2>
      <ul class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-disc">
        <li><strong>[DATE]:</strong> The date you're sending or posting the notice. Not the date you drafted it. Courts look at the date on the letter when calculating whether you gave sufficient notice.</li>
        <li><strong>[TENANT FULL NAME(S)]:</strong> Exactly as they appear on the lease. Nicknames and shortened names create ambiguity.</li>
        <li><strong>[PROPERTY ADDRESS]:</strong> Full street address, unit number, city, state, ZIP. Omitting the unit number in a multi-unit building may make the notice defective.</li>
        <li><strong>[LEASE START DATE] and [LEASE EXPIRATION DATE]:</strong> Both come from the lease agreement. If there have been amendments, use the most recent version's dates.</li>
        <li><strong>[MOVE-OUT DATE]:</strong> In most cases, this equals the lease expiration date.</li>
        <li><strong>[$SECURITY DEPOSIT AMOUNT]:</strong> The total deposit held, including any additional deposits (pet deposit, etc.).</li>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> Sign in ink. If signing as an agent for an LLC or corporation, sign "[YOUR NAME], as authorized agent for [ENTITY NAME]."</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">State-Specific Requirements: Why a Generic Template Can Fail</h2>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]">A non-renewal letter that omits state-specific language or is sent outside the required notice window may be entirely void. If a tenant stays past the lease date and you try to evict them, a defective notice can get your case dismissed before it starts.</p>
      <table class="w-full border-collapse my-6">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2 text-left font-heading font-semibold text-brand-heading bg-brand-bg">State</th>
            <th class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2 text-left font-heading font-semibold text-brand-heading bg-brand-bg">Key Requirement</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2"><strong>California</strong></td>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2">Tenancies over 1 year require 60 days notice. AB 1482 properties require "just cause" after 12 months. Cities like LA, SF, and Oakland have additional local rules.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2"><strong>New York</strong></td>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2">Less than 1 year: 30 days. 1–2 years: 60 days. 2+ years: 90 days. HSTPA expanded tenant protections.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2"><strong>New Jersey</strong></td>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2">The Anti-Eviction Act protects many tenants from non-renewal without just cause.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2"><strong>Oregon</strong></td>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2">90 days notice for most non-renewals. Portland and some areas require relocation assistance for no-cause non-renewals.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2"><strong>Texas</strong></td>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2">Month-to-month: 30 days. Fixed-term: per lease terms (typically 30–60 days). No statewide just-cause requirement.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2"><strong>Florida</strong></td>
            <td class="border border-brand-border px-3 py-2">Year-to-year: 60 days. Quarter-to-quarter: 30 days. Month-to-month: 15 days. Week-to-week: 7 days.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">Common Mistakes Landlords Make Completing Templates</h2>
      <ul class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-disc">
        <li><strong>Wrong date.</strong> Use the date you're actually serving or mailing—not the draft date.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing or wrong unit number.</strong> Always include the full address as it appears on the lease.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrong tenant name spelling or partial names.</strong> Copy names character by character from the original signed lease.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrong notice period calculated.</strong> Calculate the required period before you fill in any dates.</li>
        <li><strong>The landlord name doesn't match the property owner.</strong> If the property is owned by an LLC, the notice must come from that entity.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">Invalid Wording That Gets Notices Thrown Out</h2>
      <ul class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-disc">
        <li>"We are considering not renewing your lease" — conditional, not a notice</li>
        <li>"Your lease may expire soon" — vague, not formal notice of intent</li>
        <li>"Please be advised that we will need you to vacate" — reads as a demand to vacate, not a non-renewal</li>
        <li>"As soon as possible" — not a legal date; must state a specific calendar date</li>
        <li>"Unless other arrangements are made" — introduces conditions that undermine finality</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">Pre-Serve Checklist: Before You Send This Notice</h2>
      <ol class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-decimal">
        <li>Confirm the notice period from your state's current statute</li>
        <li>Check for local ordinances that may require more than state law</li>
        <li>Verify just-cause requirements for your property</li>
        <li>Pull the original signed lease and match tenant names exactly</li>
        <li>Confirm the lease expiration date from the most recent version</li>
        <li>Calculate the notice period from today's service date</li>
        <li>Verify the move-out date is correct for your tenancy type</li>
        <li>Confirm the landlord/entity name matches the property owner of record</li>
        <li>Prepare two envelopes: certified mail and regular first-class mail</li>
        <li>Photocopy or scan the signed notice before it leaves your hands</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">For Month-to-Month vs. Fixed-Term Leases: What Changes</h2>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]"><strong>Fixed-term lease non-renewal:</strong> The lease expiration date is already established—you're confirming it won't be extended. The notice deadline counts back from the expiration date already in the lease.</p>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]"><strong>Month-to-month lease non-renewal:</strong> There's no predetermined end date—the notice creates one. The move-out date is typically at the end of the current rental period. You must count the notice period forward from the service date.</p>
      <p class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 leading-[1.7]">For month-to-month tenancies, replace the lease expiration date language with: <em>"Your month-to-month tenancy will terminate at the end of the rental period beginning [DATE], on [FINAL DATE]. You are required to vacate the premises no later than [FINAL DATE]."</em> For guidance on the exact tone and wording to use when addressing the tenant directly, see <a href="/non-renewal-lease-letter/to-tenant/" class="text-brand-cta hover:underline font-semibold">non-renewal lease letter to tenant</a>.</p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mt-10 mb-4">After You Send the Letter: What Comes Next</h2>
      <ul class="font-body text-body-base text-brand-body mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-disc">
        <li><strong>Keep everything.</strong> Retain a copy of the signed letter, the certified mail tracking information, and the signed return receipt when it arrives. If the tenant doesn't vacate after the lease expires and owes back rent, an <a href="/eviction-notice/non-payment-of-rent/" class="text-brand-cta hover:underline font-semibold">eviction notice for non-payment of rent</a> will be your next step.</li>
        <li><strong>Mark your calendar.</strong> Set reminders for the move-out date and 3–5 days before.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not accept rent.</strong> Accepting rent after the lease expires typically creates a new month-to-month tenancy in most states.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare for holdover scenarios.</strong> Your non-renewal notice is the foundational document for a holdover eviction case.</li>
        <li><strong>Schedule the move-out inspection.</strong> Document the condition with photographs and complete the security deposit accounting within your state's required timeframe.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- CTA from content file -->
      <div class="my-10 text-center">
        <a href="/generate" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-btn-x py-btn-y bg-brand-cta hover:bg-brand-cta-dark text-white font-heading text-label font-semibold rounded-md shadow-md transition-colors duration-200 no-underline">
          Get Your Free Template — Download PDF for $14.99
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ accordion -->
      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-semibold text-brand-heading mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">Is there a difference between a non-renewal lease letter and a Notice to Vacate?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">These terms are sometimes used interchangeably, but they serve different legal purposes. A non-renewal lease letter tells a tenant the lease will end at its scheduled expiration—it's sent before or at the expiration date. A Notice to Vacate is typically used to demand that a tenant leave due to a lease violation, non-payment, or after a lease has already expired. The legal requirements and notice periods for each can differ by state.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">Can I write my own non-renewal letter or do I need an attorney?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">You can write your own non-renewal letter. Most states don't require a specific form—they require specific content and timely delivery. However, if you're non-renewing in a rent-controlled city, have a tenant who may dispute the notice, or if significant money is at stake, having an attorney draft or review the letter is worthwhile. At minimum, use a state-specific template rather than a generic one.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">Does the template work for all US states?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">The general template on this page captures the elements required in most states. However, notice periods, just-cause requirements, and delivery rules vary by state and sometimes by city. Before sending, verify your state's notice period and any local ordinances. Our generator handles state-specific requirements automatically.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">What if my tenant has been month-to-month for years—do I need a different template?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">The template structure is similar, but the language must reflect the month-to-month nature of the tenancy (no fixed expiration date) and calculate the termination date based on your state's notice requirement and the rental period. Several states also require more notice for longer-tenured tenants—California requires 60 days instead of 30 for tenancies over 1 year.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">Can I use this template for a commercial lease?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">No. Commercial leases are governed by contract terms, not residential tenant-protection statutes. The notice requirements, legal standards, and consequences of non-renewal are entirely different for commercial tenancies. Use a commercial lease attorney for commercial non-renewal notices.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">Should I include a reason for non-renewal in the letter?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">In most states, you're not required to. Including a reason can occasionally work against you—if you state a reason and the tenant challenges it, you're defending that specific reason in court. Many landlord attorneys recommend sending the notice without a stated reason unless your jurisdiction requires one. Internally document your reason in case a discrimination or retaliation claim arises later.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">What if the tenant signs a new lease before the non-renewal date?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">If both parties sign a new lease agreement, the non-renewal notice is effectively superseded. The new lease governs the tenancy going forward. If you change your mind about non-renewing, put the new agreement in writing before the old lease expires.</div>
          </details>
          <details class="border border-brand-border rounded-md">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold cursor-pointer py-3 px-4 text-brand-heading">How do I prove I sent the non-renewal letter?</summary>
            <div class="px-4 pb-4 font-body text-body-base text-brand-body">Send it via USPS Certified Mail with Return Receipt Requested and keep the green card. Also send a copy via regular first-class mail in case the tenant refuses the certified mail. Retain the letter itself, the certified mail receipt, and the tracking history. If you delivered by hand, have a witness sign and date a confirmation of delivery.</div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-brand-border">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-h3 font-semibold text-brand-heading mb-4">Related Guides</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 font-body text-body-base">
          <li><a href="/non-renewal-lease-letter/" class="text-brand-cta hover:underline font-semibold">Non-Renewal Lease Letter Guide</a> — The complete guide: when to send, what to include, state notice periods, and common mistakes.</li>
          <li><a href="/non-renewal-lease-letter/to-tenant/" class="text-brand-cta hover:underline font-semibold">Non-Renewal Lease Letter to Tenant</a> — How to address the letter directly to your tenant, with sample letters and delivery guidance.</li>
          <li><a href="/eviction-notice/non-payment-of-rent/" class="text-brand-cta hover:underline font-semibold">Eviction Notice for Non-Payment of Rent</a> — If rent goes unpaid before the lease ends, here's the correct notice to serve.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

